================================================================================
MODEL OVERVIEW
================================================================================

MODEL: Windspeed (WS) Sector-Level Uncertainty Model
-----------------------------------------------------
Target: Signed relative windspeed deviation between cross-prediction and actual
Formula: e = (WS_predicted - WS_actual) / WS_actual
Level: Sector (12 sectors per pair)
Distribution: Student-t (robust to outliers, learned v ~ 9)

Model structure:
    e ~ StudentT(v, mu=0, sigma)

    log(sigma) = log_sigma0 + SUM(gamma_i * z_i) + pair_effect

    Where:
    - v = degrees of freedom (learned, v ~ 9, moderately heavy tails)
    - log_sigma0 = baseline log-sigma
    - gamma_i = effect of each driver (HalfNormal prior, always >= 0)
    - z_i = standardized (z-scored) driver values
    - pair_effect ~ Normal(0, sigma_pair), random effect per pair

Key design choices:
    - HalfNormal priors on gamma: features can only INCREASE sigma, never
      decrease it. This encodes the physical intuition that more complexity,
      more distance, more mismatch -> more uncertainty. Never less.
    - Student-t likelihood: handles outlier sectors without distorting the model.
    - Signed errors: captures both over- and under-prediction, enabling
      +/- confidence intervals for industry communication.

Training data: 492 sectors from 41 directional pairs (~22 physical pairs)
Excluded masts: 2021PA013, 2022PA020, 2023PA059, 2015WM018, 2021PA004, 2022PA018

Note on |dTI| (removed):
    |dTI| = |TI_WTG - TI_MM| was originally in the model but was removed because
    TI at the WTG location is NEVER available at deployment (only the predictor
    mast has measurements). TI_MM is kept because it uses MM-site data only.

Note on directional pairs:
    Each physical pair (e.g., mast A and mast B) can appear as two directional
    pairs: A->B (predicting B from A) and B->A (predicting A from B).
    These share the same distance, terrain, etc. but differ in energy weighting.
    LOO cross-validation holds out BOTH directions simultaneously to avoid
    data leakage.

