================================================================================
PART 4: KEY FINDINGS FROM FEATURE EXPLORATION
================================================================================

1. TERRAIN CONFOUNDING IS EVERYWHERE
   Many features that appear significant at first glance are actually
   proxies for terrain complexity. The standard test protocol became:
   - Compute raw correlation with |error|
   - Stratify by terrain type (Flat < 40 RIX, Complex > 50 RIX)
   - Compute partial correlation controlling for dist_norm and RIX
   If the signal vanishes in flat terrain or after partial correlation,
   the feature is confounded and should not be included.

2. DISTANCE IS A PROXY, NOT A DRIVER
   The WAsP Similarity Principle (Landberg et al. 2003) states that
   site similarity -- not distance -- determines prediction quality.
   Distance captures unmeasured dissimilarities that correlate with
   separation. The terrain-normalised version (dist_norm) is a
   compromise: it encodes what we can measure (terrain type via distance_A)
   while still acting as a catch-all for what we cannot.

3. GRADIENTS > ABSOLUTE VALUES
   Turning gradient (rank #2) outperforms raw turning (rank #18).
   The rate of change between adjacent sectors is more informative than
   the absolute magnitude, because it indicates direction-dependent
   terrain interactions that are hard to model.

4. WEIBULL PARAMETERS CARRY INDEPENDENT INFORMATION
   k_deviation is not redundant with speedup or turning features (posterior
   correlation < 0.3). It captures distribution shape information that
   the terrain-derived features miss.

5. LESS IS MORE
   The 6-feature model (Top 5 + TI_MM) has BETTER Spearman correlation
   (0.846 vs 0.749) and lower bias (+1.0pp vs +2.1pp) than the full
   21-feature model. With only ~22 independent physical pairs, most
   features are noise passengers that hurt out-of-sample performance.


================================================================================
PART 5: GLOSSARY
================================================================================

Cross-prediction:
    Predicting wind conditions at one location (WTG) using measurements
    from another location (MM), adjusted for terrain differences.

dz:
    Height difference between WTG and MM. Positive = WTG is higher.

Energy weight:
    Fraction of total energy production attributed to each sector.
    Based on wind frequency and windspeed (power ~ v^3).

Flip fraction:
    When wind is deflected (turning), energy "flips" from one sector to
    another. This fraction measures how much energy is "borrowed" from
    adjacent sectors.

MM (Measurement Mast):
    The location where wind is measured (predictor site).

RIX (Ruggedness Index):
    Percentage of terrain within a radius exceeding a slope threshold.
    RIX_0.3 uses 30% slope (~17 deg), indicating "severe" terrain.
    RIX_0.0501 uses ~5% slope (~3 deg), indicating any non-flat terrain.

Sector:
    One of 12 wind directions, each spanning 30 deg. Centred on compass
    points: N (345-15 deg), NNE (15-45 deg), etc.

Speedup factor:
    How terrain affects wind speed relative to a reference condition.
    >1 = acceleration (hilltops), <1 = deceleration (valleys).

WAsP:
    Wind Atlas Analysis and Application Program. Industry-standard
    software for wind resource assessment based on linearised flow models.

WTG (Wind Turbine Generator):
    The location where wind conditions are being predicted (predicted site).
    In our context, this is where the actual measurement exists for validation.

Weibull distribution:
    Standard parametric distribution for wind speeds, described by:
    - A (scale parameter): related to mean wind speed
    - k (shape parameter): describes the "peakedness"
      k ~ 2: typical, moderate variability
      k > 3: narrow speed range (channelling)
      k < 1.5: wide speed range (variable/gusty)


